   - A digital lowpass filter, as some remasters use, gives the same picture as an encoder's lowpass.
   - Also: a wall at 14.3 kHz with digital silence above: also where a 32 kHz source ends.
   - Also: tone at 15.75 kHz: TV line frequency (TV simulcast, or a TV set near the recorder).
   - But a noise reducer opens and closes the top bands with the music too. After an encoder's lowpass the digital silence above starts at the cutoff, frame after frame; here that holds in 0 % of the frames, so these holes are not paired with any other clue.
   - Digital silence above the 14.3 kHz lowpass: nothing analog came after it.
   - Digital silence above the 16.0 kHz lowpass: nothing analog came after it.
   - Encoder holes are the fingerprint of a perceptual encoder (MP3, MP2, AAC switch the top bands off and on); FM and analog recordings stay under about 5 dB.
   - Encoder holes of 19 dB below the 16.0 kHz lowpass.
   - Encoder-like holes of 16 dB under the 14.3 kHz treble cut, but the silence above does not follow a fixed lowpass: a noise reducer can leave this too.
   - High frequencies are present (-0 dB relative to 1-4 kHz above 15 kHz), so encoder traces would show.
   - High frequencies are present (-0 dB relative to 1-4 kHz above 15 kHz), so encoder traces would show.
   - High frequencies are present (-0 dB relative to 1-4 kHz above 15 kHz), so encoder traces would show.
   - No MP3 or AAC coding traces were found. An MP3 or AAC converted straight back to a lossless format shows them, even at high bitrates; audio processed after decoding (resampled, equalised) or another codec would not, so the doubt is smaller but remains.
   - No brick-wall lowpass: lossy encoders always cut the top of the spectrum.
   - No brick-wall lowpass: lossy encoders always cut the top of the spectrum.
   - No brick-wall lowpass: lossy encoders always cut the top of the spectrum.
   - The edge of the lowpass is sharp (3264 dB per octave), as encoders and digital filters cut (a hint only).
   - These two clues measure different things (how the top bands behave from frame to frame; what came after the lowpass), and a lossy encoder leaves both: together they prove a lossy source.
   - This is one family of clues only: a doubt, not a proof. A second, independent clue (encoder holes with digital silence above, intensity stereo, coding traces) would make it certain. Compare with other versions of the show if you can.
  A lossy file converted back has digital silence there (about 0 dB). Steady hiss well above 0 dB was added by an analog stage (tuner, sound card) after any encoder.
  A lossy file converted back has digital silence there (about 0 dB). Steady hiss well above 0 dB was added by an analog stage (tuner, sound card) after any encoder.
  A true hi-res recording has content (or at least hiss) up there; an upsampled 44.1/48 kHz file is empty above 22-24 kHz, and so is an analog source with nothing up there transferred at a high rate.
  An FM or analog capture keeps its hiss up to the converter's filter (+20 dB and more); an encoder after that stage leaves silence there.
  An FM or analog capture keeps its hiss up to the converter's filter (+20 dB and more); an encoder after that stage leaves silence there.
  An encoder's or a digital filter's lowpass is usually a wall: the level falls steeply from one band to the next and nothing of the music is left above it. Tape, microphones and analog filters roll off gradually (16 dB or less) and the band above still follows the music (0.6 and more). Lossy files can roll off gradually too: a hint, never a proof.
