3. Encoder holes are the fingerprint of a perceptual encoder (MP3, MP2, AAC switch the top bands off and on); FM and analog recordings stay under about 5 dB.
3. These two clues measure different things (how the top bands behave from frame to frame; what came after the lowpass), and a lossy encoder leaves both: together they prove a lossy source.
4. But a noise reducer opens and closes the top bands with the music too. After an encoder's lowpass the digital silence above starts at the cutoff, frame after frame; here that holds in 0 % of the frames, so these holes are not paired with any other clue.
5. A digital lowpass filter, as some remasters use, gives the same picture as an encoder's lowpass.
6. This is one family of clues only: a doubt, not a proof. A second, independent clue (encoder holes with digital silence above, intensity stereo, coding traces) would make it certain. Compare with other versions of the show if you can.
7. Also: a wall at 14.3 kHz with digital silence above: also where a 32 kHz source ends.
8. Also: tone at 15.75 kHz: TV line frequency (TV simulcast, or a TV set near the recorder).
9 file(s) checked for lossless origin: 7 OK, 1 warning(s), 1 error(s)
9. No MP3 or AAC coding traces were found. An MP3 or AAC converted straight back to a lossless format shows them, even at high bitrates; audio processed after decoding (resampled, equalised) or another codec would not, so the doubt is smaller but remains.
